  Why our exporter sees an empty eye material: The chain AppearanceMaterial →
  SubObjectAppearance → snoMaterial → MaterialDefinition → tUberMaterial → ptMatTexList
  is the only path our material_parser walks. For Hero_Eye textures it reaches
  global_eyeball_mat and finds an empty ptMatTexList. The textures are bound one level
  deeper, in the Hero_Eye.shd shader file's hardcoded sampler bindings — that's outside
  the standard material chain.

  Implementation options for glTF export

  Three plausible approaches if you want eyes textured:

  1. Static base-eye textures with default tint (simple): hardcode-embed the 8
  base_eyes_* textures whenever shader_map == "Hero_Eye", ship them as the eyeball's
  BASE_COLOR + NORMAL. The result is generic blue eyes (since base_eyes_irisColor is
  grayscale and we'd skip the iris-color tint). Crude but functional.
  2. EyeColor-preset baking (more involved): pick an EyeColor preset (e.g. Brown
  Medium), composite base_eyes_irisColor × rgbaIrisInner + sclera + iris mask + limbus
  into a single 1024×1024 RGBA image at export time, embed as the eyeball's BASE_COLOR.
  Needs a small compositor.
  3. Set baseColorFactor from preset only (current Lilith approach): pick a preset and
  use its rgbaIrisInner directly as the eyeball material's baseColorFactor. No texture,
  but at least the eye colour roughly matches the preset. Same shape as our
  Iris_InnerColor_Eye fallback for Lilith — just keyed on the EyeColor SNO group instead
   of ptRunTimeMaterialValues.

  Option 3 is the smallest change and aligns with the precedent we set for Lilith.
  Option 1 gives more fidelity at the cost of always-blue eyes for everyone. Option 2 is
   the proper fix but requires a small shader-emulation pass.

  For now I'd leave eyeball materials at white (the current behaviour) and revisit when
  you want player-character exports to look complete — at that point Option 3 is one
  extra --eye-color-preset Brown\ Medium CLI flag away.